---
import FieldGuideLayout from '../../../components/FieldGuideLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<FieldGuideLayout
  title="ROM file structure"
  description="Where everything lives in the NDS ROM — folders, file counts, and what each directory contains."
  crumbs={[
    { label: 'Field Guide', href: `${base}/field-guide/` },
    { label: 'File Formats', href: `${base}/field-guide/file-formats/` },
    { label: 'ROM file structure' },
  ]}
>
  <p>
    The NDS ROM for <em>Tongari Boushi to Oshare na Mahou Tsukai</em> extracts
    via <code>ndstool</code> into a flat directory tree. Six root-level files
    hold the executable code and ROM metadata; all game content lives under a
    single <code>data/</code> tree with roughly 30 subdirectories.
  </p>

  <h2>Root files</h2>
  <p>
    Six files sit at the top level of the extracted ROM. These are standard NDS
    structures present in every retail cartridge.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>File</th><th>Role</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>header.bin</code></td>
        <td>ROM header</td>
        <td>Standard 512-byte NDS header (game code, icon offset, FAT/FNT pointers, etc.)</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>arm9.bin</code></td>
        <td>Main executable</td>
        <td>BLZ-compressed. 564 KB on-ROM, 723 KB decompressed. Contains the game engine, string tables, and ARM9 overlay dispatch.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>arm7.bin</code></td>
        <td>Sub-processor executable</td>
        <td>ARM7 coprocessor code (audio, Wi-Fi, touch). Unmodified by the translation.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>y9.bin</code></td>
        <td>ARM9 overlay table</td>
        <td>Describes all 180 overlays: RAM load address, compressed size, decompressed size, BLZ flags.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>y7.bin</code></td>
        <td>ARM7 overlay table</td>
        <td>Same structure as y9.bin but for the ARM7 side. Empty in this game (no ARM7 overlays).</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>banner.bin</code></td>
        <td>Icon and title strings</td>
        <td>The 32x32 icon shown in the DS menu, plus the game title in each language slot.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Overlay directory</h2>
  <p>
    <code>overlay/</code> contains 180 <code>.bin</code> files
    (<code>overlay_0000.bin</code> through <code>overlay_0179.bin</code>).
    Each is a BLZ-compressed ARM9 code module that loads into a specific RAM
    slot at runtime. Overlays implement individual game systems: minigames,
    menus, NPC AI routines, shop logic, crafting, and so on. The overlay table
    in <code>y9.bin</code> maps each overlay ID to its load address and size.
    See the overlay system page for the full decoded function inventory.
  </p>

  <h2>Data directory overview</h2>
  <p>
    <code>data/</code> is the main content tree. It holds around 2,500 files
    organized into purpose-specific subdirectories. Most files are either
    <code>.ofs</code> (raw RESO containers) or <code>.lzs</code> (LZS-wrapped
    LZ11-compressed RESO containers). A handful are raw <code>.bin</code>,
    <code>.dat</code>, or domain-specific formats.
  </p>

  <h3>2d/</h3>
  <p>
    45 files. UI sprites, fonts, and animation resources. A mix of
    <code>.lzs</code> compressed containers and <code>.ofs</code> RESO
    containers. Examples: <code>audition.lzs</code>, <code>bank_in.lzs</code>,
    <code>buyitem.lzs</code>, and a <code>selwindow/</code> subdirectory. The
    2D art pipeline for menus and HUD elements pulls from these files.
  </p>

  <h3>charmake/</h3>
  <p>
    2 <code>.ofs</code> files. Character creation UI resources (the screens
    where the player designs their avatar at game start).
  </p>

  <h3>coordinate/</h3>
  <p>
    6 files. Coordinate/clothing system resources. <code>crditype.ofs</code>
    contains 20 SZL-packed sprite sets used by the outfit selection UI.
  </p>

  <h3>inputstr/</h3>
  <p>
    5 files. Text input UI resources (the keyboard/character picker screens).
  </p>

  <h3>item/</h3>
  <p>
    14 files. The item database: <code>itemicon.ofs</code> (656 item icons as
    packed sprites), <code>ItemNoList.itnt</code>, item tables
    (<code>.itnt</code>, <code>.dat</code>), and binary lookup files that map
    item IDs to names and categories.
  </p>

  <h3>magazine/</h3>
  <p>
    66 files. 65 <code>.ofs</code> containers (one per in-game magazine issue)
    plus 1 <code>.lzs</code>. Magazine composition data and pre-rendered page
    layouts for the in-game collectible magazine system.
  </p>

  <h3>magic/</h3>
  <p>
    2 <code>.lzs</code> files. Magic system data. <code>magic.lzs</code>
    contains the spell database with effect banks, glyph mappings, and recipe
    data for the wand-drawing system.
  </p>

  <h3>map/</h3>
  <p>
    40 files across 13 subdirectories:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Subdirectory</th><th>Contents</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>attribute/</code></td><td>Tile attribute flags</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>collision/</code></td><td>Collision geometry</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>event_rect/</code></td><td>Event trigger rectangles</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>field/</code></td><td>Field map definitions</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>fish/</code></td><td>Fish spawn point tables</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>insect/</code></td><td>Insect spawn point tables</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>mapitem/</code></td><td>Map item placement</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>mushroom/</code></td><td>Mushroom spawn point tables</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>plane/</code></td><td>Ground plane geometry</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>rep/</code></td><td>Map representation data</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>route/</code></td><td>NPC walking routes</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>shop/</code></td><td>Shop zone boundaries</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>weather/</code></td><td>Weather region definitions</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>message/</h3>
  <p>
    Approximately 1,700 <code>.ofs</code> files in a deep category tree. This
    is where ALL game dialog lives. The directory hierarchy organizes messages
    by function:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Range</th><th>Contents</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>msg00</code> - <code>msg12</code></td><td>System and event dialog</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>msg20</code> - <code>msg31</code></td><td>Per-NPC personality dialog (12 directories, ~45 files each)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>msg32</code> - <code>msg33</code></td><td>Grouped dialog</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>msg40</code> - <code>msg52</code></td><td>Large groups with 11 subdirectories each; classroom and lesson content</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>msg98</code> - <code>msg99</code></td><td>System lookups, UI labels, crafting tables, encyclopedia text</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>minigame/</h3>
  <p>5 files. Minigame-specific resources.</p>

  <h3>model/</h3>
  <p>
    Approximately 200 files across several sub-trees holding all 3D assets:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Subdirectory</th><th>Contents</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>item/</code></td><td>Item 3D models</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>map/</code></td><td>World geometry: <code>ground/</code>, <code>obj/</code>, <code>plane/</code>, <code>fish/</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>npc/</code></td><td>NPC models and textures, including <code>tex/face/</code> for face meshes</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>player/</code></td><td>Player character models</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>shop/</code></td><td>Shop interior models</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>ucc/</code></td><td>User-created content models</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>uma/</code></td><td>Horse models</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>meffect/</code></td><td>Magic effects: <code>mefftex.ofs</code> (854 BTX0 textures), <code>meffect.ofs</code> (854 BMD0 models)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>odori/</code></td><td>Dance animations</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>mydesign/</h3>
  <p>2 files. Player custom design system resources.</p>

  <h3>npc/</h3>
  <p>
    29 files. The NPC data layer:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>NpcData.lzs</code> -- master NPC database with names</li>
    <li><code>NpcDataOfs.ofs</code> -- 252 SZL-packed personality records</li>
    <li><code>NpcCstm.ofs</code> -- 140 customization parameter sets</li>
    <li><code>NpcFas.npfs</code>, <code>NpcClothInfo.nci</code>, <code>NpcSchedule.init</code> -- fashion, clothing info, daily schedules</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>npcstamp/</h3>
  <p>
    2 files. <code>npcstamp.ofs</code> (140 SZL-packed RGCN personality stamps
    for the rumor board) and <code>npcstpcmn.lzs</code> (shared NCER + NCLR
    palette data).
  </p>

  <h3>onemsg/</h3>
  <p>
    12 <code>.ofs</code> files. One-shot floating messages rendered as speech
    bubbles above characters' heads. These tooltip/hint messages have a 32-character
    page cap.
  </p>

  <h3>pc_shop/</h3>
  <p>
    14 files across subdirectories: <code>foodmenu/</code>,
    <code>redecoration/inner/</code>, <code>redecoration/outer/</code>,
    <code>selling/</code>. Shop menu layout and pricing data.
  </p>

  <h3>recipe/</h3>
  <p>
    3 files. The crafting recipe system:
    <code>recipedata.lzs</code> (RESC v3.11 container with the recipe database),
    <code>recipemsg.lzs</code> (recipe narration strings),
    <code>mgcinfo.lzs</code> (magic info lookup table).
    See the <a href={`${base}/field-guide/file-formats/recipe-files/`}>recipe files</a> page.
  </p>

  <h3>script/</h3>
  <p>
    383 <code>.ofs</code> files. Game event scripts containing bytecode
    programs that drive cutscenes, triggered events, and scripted sequences.
  </p>

  <h3>shop/</h3>
  <p>2 <code>.bin</code> files. Shop inventory configuration data.</p>

  <h3>start/</h3>
  <p>13 files. Game startup and intro sequence resources.</p>

  <h3>talk/</h3>
  <p>
    1 file: <code>word.dat</code>. The banned/swear-word filter list used by
    the text input system.
  </p>

  <h3>talkstart/</h3>
  <p>
    40 <code>.ofs</code> files. Dialog trigger tables. Each file represents a
    scenario type (greeting, shopping, weather chat, etc.); records within each
    file map (scenario, zone) pairs to specific message files in
    <code>message/</code>.
  </p>

  <h3>title/</h3>
  <p>1 file. Title screen resources.</p>

  <h3>topic/</h3>
  <p>4 files. Conversation topic data for the NPC dialog system.</p>

  <h3>trendranking/</h3>
  <p>2 files. Trend ranking system resources.</p>

  <h3>ucc/</h3>
  <p>
    27+ files plus <code>act/</code>, <code>brush/</code>, and
    <code>dishup/</code> subdirectories. User-created content resources for the
    dress-up and custom item systems.
  </p>

  <h3>uwasa/</h3>
  <p>1 <code>.bin</code> file. Rumor (<em>uwasa</em>) system data.</p>

  <h3>Loose files at data/ root</h3>
  <p>
    About two dozen <code>.lzs</code> files sit directly in <code>data/</code>
    rather than in a subdirectory. These are global resources used across
    multiple game systems:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>global.lzs</code> -- 68 KB compressed, 166 KB decompressed.
      RESO v1.21 container with 53 entries including fonts, palettes, and
      shared game tables. Byte-identical between NDS and 3DS except for one
      entry.
    </li>
    <li>
      Other examples: <code>bbs.lzs</code>, <code>catchdisp.lzs</code>,
      <code>defcos.lzs</code>, <code>entryitmname.lzs</code>,
      <code>goodslist.lzs</code>, <code>inputbbs.lzs</code>,
      <code>inputmail.lzs</code>, <code>letter.lzs</code>,
      <code>locker.lzs</code>, <code>mailorder.lzs</code>,
      <code>makeprofile.lzs</code>.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>sound_data.sdat</h3>
  <p>
    33 MB. Nintendo SDAT sound bank containing all game audio: background music,
    sound effects, and jingles. Standard SDAT format shared across most DS games.
  </p>

  <h2>File addressing at runtime</h2>
  <p>
    The game engine never references files by path at runtime. Instead, it uses
    numeric file IDs from the NDS File Allocation Table (FAT). The File Name
    Table (FNT) maps these IDs back to human-readable paths at extraction time,
    but the running code only ever works with ID numbers.
  </p>
  <p>Key file ID ranges:</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>ID range</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>511 - 1838</td><td>Message files (<code>data/message/</code>)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2122 - 2133</td><td>One-shot messages (<code>data/onemsg/</code>)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2151 - 2533</td><td>Script files (<code>data/script/</code>)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2550 - 2589</td><td>Talkstart files (<code>data/talkstart/</code>)</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    This means that adding or removing files from the ROM would shift every ID
    above the insertion point, breaking all hardcoded references in the ARM9
    binary and overlays. The translation patch modifies file contents in place
    without adding or removing any files.
  </p>

  <h2>3DS version (TBAF archives)</h2>
  <p>
    The 3DS port of the game uses TBAF (<code>.tarc</code>) archives instead of
    the NDS flat directory layout. TBAF is a simple archive format that bundles
    the same underlying game data into larger container files. The actual file
    formats inside the archives are identical to the NDS versions --
    <code>.ofs</code> RESO containers, <code>.lzs</code> compressed wrappers,
    and the same Nitro graphics formats.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>global.lzs</code> is byte-identical between the NDS and 3DS ROMs
    except for a single entry within the RESO container. The translation patch
    targets the NDS ROM; the 3DS version is a separate extraction and
    repackaging problem due to the TBAF wrapper.
  </p>

  <h2>Cross-references</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href={`${base}/field-guide/file-formats/reso/`}>RESO containers</a> -- the container format used by <code>.ofs</code> and <code>.lzs</code> files</li>
    <li><a href={`${base}/field-guide/file-formats/lz11/`}>LZS / LZ11 compression</a> -- the compression wrapper around most assets</li>
    <li><a href={`${base}/field-guide/file-formats/blz-arm9/`}>BLZ and the plaintext-ARM9 trick</a> -- compression used on <code>arm9.bin</code> and overlays</li>
    <li><a href={`${base}/field-guide/file-formats/recipe-files/`}>The recipe/ folder</a> -- detailed breakdown of the three recipe files</li>
  </ul>
</FieldGuideLayout>
